So I've spent a bit of time doing the line management thing and have therefore had the Official
Corporate Training, which like any Official Corporate Training happens somewhat
after you've started doing it#footnote["Hi, you're now a line manager, we'll send
you on the Various Training Courses in a couple of months."]). But there are other
places I've picked up things too.

= Lession ye first

In my copious free time I enjoy hillwalking#footnote[Opportunities to do it in East Anglia are
for some reason a little bit limited, but back in my youth I lived in the frozen North.]. My wife
and I had the great idea that we'd climb
#link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helvellyn#The_eastern_ridges")[Helvellyn via Striding Edge].
Now one of the things I Do Not Do is "heights", but by dint of willpower and holding on to the mountain
with my teeth I made it along Striding Edge to where it broadens out (a bit) and then there's
a bit of a scramble up to the summit.

But at the base of that scramble, where the route was a little unclear, there was a bunch of people
milling about unsure where to go. I was _similarly_ unsure where to go, but I knew that whatever I was
going to do I was not going to stay where I was. So I went straight up.

And the folks milling about, unsure what to do, followed.

I think, in the Official Corporate Training, this is described as "clearly communicating
a team North Star".

= Lesson ye second

So I have dogs, and have therefore spent a bit of time working with them. Specifically,
I have a Doberman and a working like spaniel. Neither is an "easy" breed. Spaniels are
highly scent-driven, built to find, flush, and retrieve game, and *really* want to do that.
Dobermans were originally bred for personal protection, are highly vigilant and easily
distracted. So how do you work with either breed?

The best tool for dog training is "positive reinforcement" --- if the animal does something
good, clearly mark the moment and give the dog a reward. The theory (and pretty much the practice)
goes that the good behavior will therefore be amplified. "Positive punishment" --- doing unpleasant
things to dogs who offer unwanted behaviours --- is also a thing#footnote[
  There are also "negative reinforcement" and "negative punishment", which are more complex.
  Oh, and the words "positive", "negative", "reinforcement", and "punishment" are technical terms
  that do not mean what you think they do.
 ]. What you do and how you do it has to be dog-specific --- that which one dog finds acceptable
 reward can wildly overexcite the other, that which is _reasonable_ "positive punishment" for
 one (e.g. a stern "uh-uh") can be too harsh for the other#footnote[On a related note, I was once chatting
 with a Very Senior Leader who had the human-equivalent version of this: telling a Brit "nice job"
 is about the same as getting wildly overexcited at an American. I still think she under-egged it:
 she could probably have gone as far as "not bad".].

But that's not the whole trick. You don't wait for the finished behaviour to show up and then shower
it with praise. You decide what you want to get to, think through the approximations on the way, and
reward the dog for getting closer.

So I was therefore somewhat startled when the Official Corporate People Development Training
introduced exactly the same ideas, minus the bits on deciding on a target and purposefully building
towards it. I have never given any of my team a dried sprat, but I
will admit to always giving them problems one size bigger than the last one they solved.

Their _retrieves_ are still lousy, though.